A||N||7||Matt85_GT||ECM Help!||||5||
Z||000000||Matt85_GT||04-19-2000||05:18 PM||||I am about to put in a new ECM after finding out what I believe burned up the old one.  The instructions for the new ecm has a chart, which says to the check the resistance between certain terminals on the ecm connectors with the ecm disconnected.  They all checked out except the resistance between the A6 to A7 terminals which was supposed to be over 20 ohms and it measured just over 5 ohms.  This A7 terminal is used for the shift light on manual cars and for the transaxle convertor clutch on autos.  I was wondering if this would explain the difference or if there is some other problem.  What should I check.  Or if possible could someone check the resistance between these to terminals on a no problems V6 manual fiero to see how they compare.  Hope someone can help. Thanks, Matt||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||04-19-2000||07:23 PM||||the lamp and the TCC solinoid would likey show diferant values.<P>You should also check the following sensors for shorts to 12V or ground. Connect the meter - to ground. screw or clamp it down so it stays tight. sheck each sensor for voltage. Should all read Zero. then check resistance to ground. I don't think any sensor should show resitance to ground. If they do it sould be on only one lead.<P>TPS<BR>MAT<BR>coolant temp<BR>MAP<P>You're chart should say what terminals are what sensor.<P>If you don't need to return the old ECM I'd like to see what killed it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fret Naught||04-19-2000||07:24 PM||||Matt here is a hint, check your lead resistence,put the two leads of your meter together and measure the resistence, then deduct that from your total reading.  If you lead resistence is 5 ohms, then really you are reading a short between you two terminals  Understand? I use a real nice Fluke at work and depending on the leads, can get up to 3 ohms of lead resistence.||||reg||1
Z||000003||loafer87gt||04-20-2000||03:09 AM||||This is kinda off-topic, but is there a core charge on ECM's? When I had mine replaced at my dealership and asked for my old one back I was told it was shipped off to GM to be rebuilt. It seems kinda strange that they would pay someone to fix something that probably cost 5 bucks to produce. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Matt85_GT||04-20-2000||09:18 AM||||I tested the lead resistance and got .2 to .3 ohms <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||04-20-2000||11:41 AM||||Depends where you get it. Most are returned to the vendor. GM may not fix it, but may sell the dead ones to someone who does.||||reg||1
Z||000006||daved||04-20-2000||08:05 PM||||Matt, ECM connector C2, term A7 shows the ECM switching to ground either the shift light, or the Auto tranny clutch. Cicuit #455 & 422. <BR>I would expect the Torque Converter Clutch Solenoid to read fairly low as it is supplied by a 10Amp fuse, which also supplies the gauges (Haynes pg.279).  I would think what you measured is o/k...but there is something else wrong if you are sure this is the correct cicuit #422 (A7 ECM to ground).  If you have the correct circuit and an auto tranny, the solenoid can only be energised when the transmission reaches 3rd. gear as there is a switch inside the tranny. You should not be able to measure anything (Megohms or infinity..out of range) when the tranny is not operating. And in any case, the resistance would appear from the +12 Volt side as it is fed from a fuse. (Remove the fuse before measuring to protect your meter)<BR>If you have the Manual tranny, then you will measure the lamp bulb resistance in a similar fashion. (Haynes shows BOTH as term A7...could this be the same terminal?)<P>Hope this helps, Dave<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||04-21-2000||02:43 PM||||I'd have to look at my GM ECM paperwork but that terminal doing 2 jobs sounds right. It think it's like that on all Fiero ECM's.||||reg||1
